00:48:26,240 --> 00:48:28,320
It's an extremely busy space.

590
00:48:28,720 --> 00:48:28,800
Yeah.

591
00:48:29,520 --> 00:48:32,880
Extremely noisy space on the App Store, but there's just so much.

592
00:48:33,680 --> 00:48:35,920
There are so many of them that are full of ads.

593
00:48:36,000 --> 00:48:44,320
There are a lot of them that are really focused on widgets, but sort of the actual in-app experience is a little bit cumbersome and just like not really interesting.

594
00:48:46,560 --> 00:48:49,600
Yeah, a lot of them are just really old and not really maintained anymore.

595
00:48:50,000 --> 00:48:54,320
So anyways, I just never really found anything that tickled me.

596
00:48:55,440 --> 00:49:03,840
So yeah, at a certain point, I was just like, well, this might be a good, I guess the other thing to mention is I was certainly looking for another fairly narrowly scoped project.

597
00:49:03,920 --> 00:49:05,040
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

598
00:49:05,840 --> 00:49:21,280
This certainly took me way longer than I thought it would, but I'm also certainly glad that it was as relatively narrow in scope as it was because it took me a year and a half to get out what is, like, at the end of the day, in some ways, a pretty simple countdown app, right?

599
00:49:21,440 --> 00:49:25,520
Like, I'm not building some huge complicated product here.

600
00:49:26,160 --> 00:49:35,760
But, like, given your, your sort of style, keeping the scope narrow allows you to spend the obnoxious amount of time on the polish.

601
00:49:35,920 --> 00:49:40,960
Because that, I mean, that's like with all of your stuff, it's like, that's what really makes it shine.

602
00:49:41,440 --> 00:49:46,320
It's not the amount of features, it's how well they're done.

603
00:49:47,200 --> 00:49:47,280
Yeah.

604
00:49:47,520 --> 00:49:51,920
And so, and so, yeah., I think I just kind of thought like a countdown app was a good vehicle for that, right?

605
00:49:52,160 --> 00:49:55,440
It's just like a slightly larger in scope than no bother.

606
00:49:56,560 --> 00:49:59,200
Also probably quite a bit more useful.

607
00:49:59,440 --> 00:50:03,280
Like it felt more like the kind of app that actually might have legs.

608
00:50:03,600 --> 00:50:07,440
I mean, the existence of a market kind of proves that out, yeah.

609
00:50:07,600 --> 00:50:11,280
So obviously the challenge there is actually getting noticed, which we can get into later.

610
00:50:11,600 --> 00:50:20,720
But yeah, it felt like a good vehicle for that next step to allow me to really polish things up, explore some interesting ideas around like a pretty simple core.

611
00:50:21,360 --> 00:50:23,200
So did you have an initial hook?

612
00:50:23,600 --> 00:50:36,000
I mean, I can already see the description of bear being a list of your events and then what sort of the main skeleton of up ahead is.

613
00:50:36,560 --> 00:50:39,280
Was that kind of the initial idea of what differentiated it?

614
00:50:39,600 --> 00:50:52,000
Yeah, the first thing that sort of piqued my interest in thinking through this and thinking through what it could be and what could make it interesting was sort of that main timeline view.

615
00:50:52,400 --> 00:51:02,880
At least all countdown apps I've ever seen, the sort of in-app experience is really just here's a list of all the events you've created that you can then., you know, spin widgets out of or whatever.

616
00:51:03,600 --> 00:51:29,440
And for me, I guess, I think part of the reason I used my silly note instead of putting widgets on my home screen is because I think what I actually liked was not just knowing that there are X days until this event I'm looking forward to, but being able to look at the future more holistically.

617
00:51:29,680 --> 00:51:31,920
Like look at the next up ahead, you might say.

618
00:51:32,400 --> 00:51:32,480
Yeah.

619
00:51:33,280 --> 00:51:36,400
Look up ahead at the next three weeks and be like, oh man.

620
00:51:36,560 --> 00:51:39,840
oh man, there's like six things that I'm looking forward to in the next three weeks.

621
00:51:40,160 --> 00:51:42,000
And here's how they all fit in together, right?

622
00:51:42,240 --> 00:51:43,840
And oh, these are like back to back to back.

623
00:51:44,000 --> 00:51:45,200
That's going to be awesome three days.

624
00:51:45,760 --> 00:51:49,040
That quickly became, I think, the sort of first differentiator for me.

625
00:51:49,200 --> 00:52:11,360
The first thing that I was trying to do is that timeline view that you see up ahead now that actually shows you visually, sort of, how many days are passing between events and gives you this really sort of attractive way to actually scroll through your future events and also scroll through your past events too, you know, to sort of look back on some of the fun things you've done over the past month or whatever it might be.

626
00:52:11,520 --> 00:52:11,920
So yeah.

627
00:52:12,000 --> 00:52:18,800
Yeah, you sort of just glossed over it, but the gap between the events being different depending on the realistic gap.

628
00:52:19,360 --> 00:52:20,400
Where did that idea come from?

629
00:52:20,960 --> 00:52:29,120
I think the very first screenshot I ever saw when you were working on this had that in there, and it was kind of like a, like, mind blowing kind of feeling.

630
00:52:29,760 --> 00:52:34,080
It's a simple concept, but Yeah, I don't know exactly.

631
00:52:35,920 --> 00:52:37,600
I think I think I was just playing.

632
00:52:38,000 --> 00:52:39,520
So here's what I will say.

633
00:52:39,680 --> 00:52:48,160
I can remember playing with a few different UI ideas that weren't that before settling on that, but were sort of versions of that.

634
00:52:48,320 --> 00:52:52,000
I can remember trying to mess around with like, what was I trying to do?

635
00:52:52,160 --> 00:52:58,400
I was trying to like draw lines so the events were stacked vertically, but I was trying to like just draw lines between them.

636
00:52:58,640 --> 00:53:07,600
But if they were, if they were further apart, I was drawing like loopy lines, like having them sort of loop around just kind of like how messages does.

637
00:53:08,160 --> 00:53:08,960
Yeah, exactly.

638
00:53:09,200 --> 00:53:12,320
I think I was, yeah, I was thinking about that a little bit.

639
00:53:12,800 --> 00:53:16,720
But no, to be honest, I don't really know exactly where that idea came from.

640
00:53:17,600 --> 00:53:24,640
At some point, it probably just occurred to me, oh, it would be cool if like there was actually if this wasn't just a normal list, if you could actually if if it if there was some.

641
00:53:25,120 --> 00:53:26,000
visual interest in here.

642
00:53:26,240 --> 00:53:42,240
And it was tricky to get right at first because initially I just kind of naively tried to, you know, do something where it's like, okay, well, so if you look at the gaps between events and up ahead, there's like these little segments, these line segments, they're kind of like dashed lines, right?

643
00:53:42,480 --> 00:53:47,360
And I was like, okay, well, I guess maybe I could have each dash be worth a day.

644
00:53:48,160 --> 00:54:00,640
But then immediately you're like, okay, well, that's going to be a terrible experience because like if I if the first event I input is like a birthday that's coming up in two hundred days, well, that's kind of a silly, uh, scrolling experience.

645
00:54:01,440 --> 00:54:14,160
So yeah, I had to kind of hone that or yeah, figure that out, figure out how to balance that where you have that visual separation, but it doesn't create just this sort of like silly annoying experience.

646
00:54:15,040 --> 00:54:15,280
Nice.

647
00:54:16,240 --> 00:54:19,360
Okay, so you have the idea of a countdown app.

648
00:54:19,920 --> 00:54:22,720
You noodle on kind of a general design.

649
00:54:24,480 --> 00:54:27,360
And then you said you spent a year on this.

650
00:54:27,760 --> 00:54:31,760
What was, I mean, that initial thing seemed like that happened pretty quick.

651
00:54:31,920 --> 00:54:34,000
This is me bringing some insider information in here.

652
00:54:34,880 --> 00:54:38,800
But the year was a pretty long process of continual advancement.

653
00:54:39,120 --> 00:54:44,000
Let's like, let's kind of like walk through a little bit, uh, how that went.

654
00:54:44,240 --> 00:54:45,440
What, what was it that took so long?

655
00:54:45,920 --> 00:54:46,000
Yeah.

656
00:54:47,920 --> 00:54:51,920
If only I knew that that sounded more tacky than I meant it.

657
00:54:54,240 --> 00:54:54,960
What indeed?

658
00:54:55,520 --> 00:54:56,240
Oh my gosh.

659
00:54:57,040 --> 00:55:01,120
Um, yeah, it's it's honestly, it's painful to look back at.

660
00:55:02,000 --> 00:55:03,840
I don't think it's my pinned tweet anymore.

661
00:55:04,000 --> 00:55:05,440
I probably changed it when we launched.

662
00:55:05,600 --> 00:55:11,200
But for a long time, the, the, my initial sort of announcement tweet of like, it's the thing you mentioned earlier.

663
00:55:11,360 --> 00:55:14,400
I'm going to save the world by building a countdown app.

664
00:55:14,960 --> 00:55:22,240
Um, if you look at the screenshot attached to that, it looks really similar to what up ahead ended up being, right?

665
00:55:22,400 --> 00:55:36,000
And so like on the surface, it's really painful to look at that and go, like, I got to basically the entire timeline, like that whole thing was built, at least, at least, yeah, on a surface level in like two weeks.

666
00:55:36,880 --> 00:55:38,080
Um, I was very excited.

667
00:55:38,160 --> 00:55:40,240
And I was like, yeah, like, look at all this progress I'm making.

668
00:55:40,320 --> 00:55:41,520
This is going to be another Oh bother, right?

669
00:55:41,680 --> 00:55:43,680
Like, I'm going to have this thing out in two months.

670
00:55:43,920 --> 00:55:45,600
And why did it take so long?

671
00:55:45,920 --> 00:55:50,240
Again, just time, uh, full-time job evenings and weekends, kid.

672
00:55:50,960 --> 00:55:52,560
There's just not a lot of time to do this stuff.

673
00:55:52,560 --> 00:55:56,880
There were large stretches of time where I just, you know, wasn't making that much progress.

674
00:55:57,920 --> 00:56:01,120
Because it's hard to balance all the things you want to do in life.

675
00:56:01,440 --> 00:56:01,520
Yeah.

676
00:56:02,320 --> 00:56:09,360
But, yeah, more concretely, I certainly wrangling dates is difficult.

677
00:56:09,760 --> 00:56:09,840
Yeah.

678
00:56:09,920 --> 00:56:10,400
That takes time.

679
00:56:10,800 --> 00:56:11,360
So I hear.

680
00:56:11,760 --> 00:56:11,840
Yeah.

681
00:56:12,080 --> 00:56:20,000
So like it's one thing to stand up an UI to the extent that you can take a screenshot of it and post it to Twitter and get some likes on it.

682
00:56:20,720 --> 00:56:24,880
But it's another thing to actually make sure all those weird edge cases are working properly?

683
00:56:25,120 --> 00:56:30,640
How are you handling recurring events and how are you handling multiday events?

684
00:56:30,800 --> 00:56:33,120
And what if an event has a time set and one of it doesn't?

685
00:56:33,360 --> 00:56:36,560
What if the start date has a time set, but for some reason the end date doesn't?

686
00:56:36,880 --> 00:56:38,800
What if an event doesn't have an end date?

687
00:56:39,440 --> 00:56:47,280
And like, and how do you actually order your timeline in such a way that that always sort of feels organic and makes sense?

688
00:56:48,640 --> 00:56:54,320
Which, if that's something, if you've used up ahead and you haven't thought about that too much, then hopefully I've done my job right.

689
00:56:54,480 --> 00:56:55,760
Because that's sort of the goal, right?

690
00:56:55,920 --> 00:56:57,600
Is it a shit complexity goal?

691
00:56:58,000 --> 00:56:58,000
Yeah.

692
00:56:59,600 --> 00:57:03,040
But yeah, that is not a trick trivial thing to get right.

693
00:57:04,640 --> 00:57:10,800
But then I think the other thing is to like, honestly, we talked about Obother earlier and how it was kind of this cute little app.

694
00:57:11,120 --> 00:57:12,080
It was very polished.

695
00:57:14,080 --> 00:57:22,640
On the other hand, I do look at that app now, and there are a lot of parts of that app that to my eyes weren't particularly polished.

696
00:57:23,040 --> 00:57:32,880
And I do think like I, I took a big extra step with Up Ahead where with Obother there were parts of it where I was just like, okay, this is good enough.

697
00:57:32,960 --> 00:57:33,680
I'm going to move on.

698
00:57:33,680 --> 00:57:35,520
I'm trying, you know, I'm trying to timebox this.

699
00:57:35,600 --> 00:57:36,480
I'm trying to get it out the door.

700
00:57:36,960 --> 00:57:37,200
Right.

701
00:57:37,680 --> 00:57:42,560
And I think with Up Ahead, I I really just tried really hard to never do that.

702
00:57:42,880 --> 00:57:44,320
I really tried hard with Up Ahead.

703
00:57:44,800 --> 00:57:58,160
Every sort of new feature I built, every new screen I built, I was really trying to kind of start from, I don't know, from first principles, I guess if I can say that, and figure out, like, how do I make this particular thing interesting?

704
00:57:58,480 --> 00:58:00,480
How do I make it actually fun, right?

705
00:58:02,000 --> 00:58:04,320
How do I tell people how to use widgets?

706
00:58:04,400 --> 00:58:04,480
Yeah.

707
00:58:04,640 --> 00:58:06,080
That's where I was going to go.

708
00:58:07,280 --> 00:58:12,960
If anyone listening has not looked at the widget settings for Up Ahead, it is really interesting.

709
00:58:12,960 --> 00:58:14,720
It is ridiculous in the best way possible.

710
00:58:16,160 --> 00:58:16,240
Yeah.

711
00:58:17,120 --> 00:58:23,040
So, yeah, I think it's just like the reason it took time is because every, every individual piece took time, right?

712
00:58:23,280 --> 00:58:27,440
It's just like I was spending a long time thinking through every screen.

713
00:58:27,680 --> 00:58:29,200
How do I want my settings to work?

714
00:58:29,440 --> 00:58:31,520
How do I want the filtering to work?

715
00:58:31,760 --> 00:58:35,360
How do I I iterated a lot on event creation, right?

716
00:58:35,680 --> 00:58:48,800
Like how do I make sure that creating events feels quick and easy and fun without but also powerful enough to not feel like there's still, there's still, you still want to give people control there, right?

717
00:58:49,200 --> 00:58:56,560
Like they're still controlling what icon they want to use and have some pretty meaningful controls around dates and the patterns.

718
00:58:56,800 --> 00:59:00,160
And so there's a, yeah, I I certainly iterated on that a lot.

719
00:59:00,640 --> 00:59:03,760
So yeah, short answer is I just think every piece of that app just took time.

720
00:59:04,320 --> 00:59:09,520
Every time I thought, oh, this will be an easy screen, this will be something I can just, like, bang out quickly, that was never true.

721
00:59:09,680 --> 00:59:13,040
I always ended up, you know, pushing myself to do something a little bit different.

722
00:59:13,600 --> 00:59:17,120
What was your, like, test flight, uh, beta strategy for this?

723
00:59:17,280 --> 00:59:22,000
Were you seeding it out to a lot of people early on or small group of people?

724
00:59:22,160 --> 00:59:24,720
Or, uh, short answer is the test flight.

725
00:59:25,360 --> 00:59:27,280
stayed pretty small until the very, very end.

726
00:59:27,920 --> 00:59:32,160
I just very slowly brought more people in as I went.

727
00:59:32,880 --> 00:59:44,640
I think I just worried about a couple of things with the test flight that probably weren't particularly well founded, but I was very cautious not to bring too many people in too quickly.

728
00:59:45,200 --> 00:59:50,400
One, because I just didn't want to accidentally break a bunch of people's apps.

729
00:59:50,560 --> 00:59:59,680
I really wanted to make sure that when a lot of people had their hands on a beta, I was really I wanted to make sure I was confident that I could transition them to the real app.

730
01:00:00,240 --> 01:00:01,520
and not like breaking their data.

731
01:00:02,000 --> 01:00:02,080
Yeah.

732
01:00:02,560 --> 01:00:05,680
You know, accidentally be like, oh, oops, all your data's gone.

733
01:00:05,760 --> 01:00:06,800
You're going to have to start again.

734
01:00:07,680 --> 01:00:09,200
So for a long time I was just worried about that.

735
01:00:09,360 --> 01:00:10,640
I don't think that ever happened.

736
01:00:11,200 --> 01:00:11,280
Yeah.

737
01:00:11,600 --> 01:00:22,800
I wanted to make sure it was, I kept it in a small enough circle that if I had to completely break something, you know, because I realized the data model was kind of fundamentally flawed or something like that, that I felt like I could.

738
01:00:23,120 --> 01:00:33,680
Anyway, so yeah, I slowly just kind of opened it up round by round to people who were showing particular interest and mostly to people who I did have some, some, like, familiarity with.

739
01:00:33,920 --> 01:00:34,000
Right.

740
01:00:34,240 --> 01:00:38,400
People who I had kind of established connections with in the past, mostly through Twitter.

741
01:00:39,680 --> 01:00:46,480
But yeah, then right at the end, I just kind of I don't think I was really planning to do this until close to the end.

742
01:00:46,720 --> 01:00:51,520
In fact, I think at one point I announced on the up ahead Twitter, I was like, I'm not going to do a public beta.

743
01:00:51,920 --> 01:00:53,600
I'm just going to push to release this thing.

744
01:00:53,760 --> 01:00:54,480
So stay tuned.

745
01:00:54,640 --> 01:00:58,240
But then at a certain point, I would, yeah, I guess I just changed my tune.

746
01:00:58,480 --> 01:01:05,840
I was like, okay, I should probably actually just get some people to exercise this a little bit before I press the launch button.

747
01:01:06,160 --> 01:01:10,400
So yeah, open it up pretty widely in a few waves and very glad I did.

748
01:01:10,720 --> 01:01:11,360
It was super helpful.

749
01:01:11,520 --> 01:01:16,000
Like I got a lot of good feedback, managed to fix a lot of little bugs that I probably wouldn't have caught otherwise.

750
01:01:16,560 --> 01:01:19,840
And I do think it created some excitement, like some buzz, right?

751
01:01:20,080 --> 01:01:22,160
To have those waves of seats opening up.

752
01:01:22,320 --> 01:01:29,520
Yeah, it definitely seemed like even during the phase before you opened it up, because I mean, I've talked about this and I've talked about it with you.

753
01:01:29,840 --> 01:01:36,880
I'm a big fan of the big beta test flights just in terms of trying to garner some attention leading up to a launch.

754
01:01:37,280 --> 01:01:40,480
There was a lot of like buzz in the like, what is this thing?

755
01:01:40,640 --> 01:01:41,600
Or how do I get access?

756
01:01:41,840 --> 01:01:46,560
And every time you tweeted something, people would be very excited and, you know, want to know what the app was.

757
01:01:46,880 --> 01:01:48,160
And so it is interesting.

758
01:01:49,040 --> 01:01:52,800
I do think part of that works because your app is so unique.

759
01:01:52,960 --> 01:01:57,200
I mean, just looking at even a visual picture of it, it definitely stands out.

760
01:01:57,360 --> 01:01:58,560
But yeah, it was interesting.

761
01:01:58,800 --> 01:02:00,320
It was interesting to see how that all played out.

762
01:02:00,480 --> 01:02:04,720
But I'm glad to hear that opening it up, uh, beforehand at least burned some fruit.

763
01:02:05,040 --> 01:02:05,120
Yeah.

764
01:02:05,280 --> 01:02:16,640
And I mean, I think the other, if I'm being honest to you, I think the other fear I had, again, probably probably not well founded, but I think I was just, you know, I saw some of that momentum.

765
01:02:16,880 --> 01:02:17,840
I saw some of that energy.

766
01:02:18,240 --> 01:02:26,560
And I think part of me just worried that like if I just opened up a public beta with ten thousand seats, everybody would get it and look at it and go, like, oh, cool, a countdown app.

767
01:02:26,720 --> 01:02:28,240
And then all that energy would kind of dry out.

768
01:02:28,560 --> 01:02:28,640
Yeah.

769
01:02:29,040 --> 01:02:29,680
But that's it.

770
01:02:29,920 --> 01:02:30,560
But I don't know.

771
01:02:30,640 --> 01:02:32,880
I don't I'm certainly not convinced that I'm right about that.

772
01:02:32,880 --> 01:02:33,360
I'm right about that.

773
01:02:34,720 --> 01:02:36,320
It could have the total opposite effect, right?

774
01:02:36,560 --> 01:02:42,000
Maybe that creates a network effect and all these people are talking with other people about this cool app that they're on the beta of.

775
01:02:42,160 --> 01:02:45,440
And I know a lot of people have had success doing betas like that in the past.

776
01:02:45,760 --> 01:02:54,080
So I think for me, ultimately, I was just kind of, I was just following my nose a little bit, it was just like, what do I feel most comfortable with?

777
01:02:54,480 --> 01:03:07,920
And for most of the development, I was just, yeah, I felt more comfortable keeping it small, just to kind of guard myself a little bit from, yeah, either breaking things for everyone or losing the momentum in my head.

778
01:03:08,560 --> 01:03:13,200
So there's a bunch of features that I would love to talk about that we definitely don't have time for.

779
01:03:13,520 --> 01:03:21,280
But one thing I do want to touch on though is the whole pattern thing and like the unlock system that you built around that.

780
01:03:21,920 --> 01:03:22,960
Where did that idea come from?

781
01:03:23,120 --> 01:03:24,640
Or I guess explain what it is first.

782
01:03:25,280 --> 01:03:35,040
Yeah, the patterns in Up Ahead I think are probably the most interesting hook and ended up being sort of the most interesting differentiator, I guess, in this app.

783
01:03:35,280 --> 01:03:48,560
Basically the way it works is, you know, so when you create an event, when you first open the app, you can set a name, you set a date, you set a color, you choose an icon, you get these nice little bubbly events on your timeline.

784
01:03:49,120 --> 01:03:53,840
But then what can happen, usually driven by the content of your events name.

785
01:03:54,320 --> 01:04:02,720
So for example, if you type in Charlie's birthday, then you'll get this pop up that says, hey, congrats, you unlocked a new pattern.

786
01:04:03,120 --> 01:04:04,640
And you'll unlock a birthday pattern.

787
01:04:04,640 --> 01:04:12,080
And basically what that will be, I don't exactly remember what's on the birthday pattern, but it's sort of like a, it's like a wallpaper that you can apply to your events, right?

